---
/**
 * The landing page's body.
 *
 * THIS WAS VARIANT A, and it won (Oleh, 2026-08-20). Three candidates shipped behind a switch on
 * 2026-08-19 so the choice could be made by reading rather than by describing: A path over product
 * screenshots with the documentation as tiles, B the same without the path, C the same with the
 * documentation as a list. The switch, the two losing components and `lib/landing-switch.ts` are
 * deleted in the same commit that inlines this one, which is what they were built for.
 *
 * WHY IT WON, as far as the evidence goes: it is the only one of the three that answered all three
 * complaints at once. It onboards (the path), it opens the roadmap (the strip), and it is not a wall
 * of links (tiles and screenshots). Its cost is height, and the measured page is 2463px against
 * B's 1978px.
 *
 * THE ORDER IS THE READER'S SEQUENCE. Which platform are you on, how do you set one up, where is
 * everything, what is coming, and how do you reach a person. The chooser leads because the landing
 * is the only screen where asking who you are is free; everywhere else the same question is a chip
 * in the sidebar that starts with everything on.
 *
 * A DRAWS CARDS, SO IT CAN ADMIT AN EMPTY ROOM. `SHOW_DRAFT_FLAGS` is that switch: a card promises
 * more than a link, and the reader is often here because a backup just failed. It is off while the
 * documentation is still filling in, and it is one line to turn on.
 *
 * THE PER-CARD GLYPH is keyed by href and falls back to the band's own, so a doc link that moves
 * loses its portrait and never loses its card.
 *
 * THE STEP STRIP NOW REACTS TO THE CHOOSER ABOVE IT, and the client's own words are the reason:
 * "we have some selector, and it just picks where to start the integration". Naming your platform
 * used to write the site-wide preference and navigate away, while every word on this page carried
 * on as if nobody had answered. Step 1 now points at that platform's connecting page and each
 * sentence takes that platform's noun, from the one `vocab` in `lib/platforms.ts`.
 *
 * NOTHING IS HIDDEN BY A CHOICE. This page is re-labelled and ADDED TO, never filtered: no section,
 * link or count is ever taken away from a reader who named a platform. Hiding is what the sidebar
 * chips do on a documentation page, where the reader is mid-task and already knows what they want.
 * On the one screen a first visitor sees, subtracting is the wrong instrument.
 *
 * TWO VARIANTS SHIP TOGETHER behind the floating switch at the foot of this file, and
 * `lib/shot-switch.ts` carries the argument for each. They differ by ONE thing: what clicking a
 * platform card does. `reactive` — the card is a door and takes you to that platform's connecting
 * page. `filter` — the card selects, the page stays, the steps take that platform's nouns and its
 * own ten pages join the documentation directory below. Both write the SAME preference, so
 * switching variant mid-session cannot leave a reader in a contradictory state.
 *
 * Both are server-rendered and the stylesheet decides which is seen, so switching costs no
 * navigation; a pre-paint inline script stamps the choice on `<html>` BEFORE this markup is parsed,
 * so the page cannot flash the other one. All of it is built to be deleted in one commit the moment
 * the client picks.
 */import { getCollection } from 'astro:content';
import { BANDS, PLATFORM_TOPICS, ROADMAP_TILES, WAYS_OUT } from '../../lib/landing';
import { STEPS, GLOSSARY_ROWS, bodyParts, nounReadings } from '../../lib/landing-steps';
import { PLATFORMS } from '../../lib/platforms';
import Icon from '../Icon.astro';
import PlatformMark from '../PlatformMark.astro';
import RequestCard from '../RequestCard.astro';
import PlatformStart from '../PlatformStart.astro';
import ShotSwitch from './ShotSwitch.astro';
import type { IconName } from '../../lib/icons';

/** See the header: on while A is a candidate would make it lose on the content, not the layout. */
const SHOW_DRAFT_FLAGS = false;

const CARD_ICON: Record<string, IconName> = {
  '/start/getting-started/': 'play',
  '/start/what-baseout-is/': 'shield-check',
  '/start/how-baseout-is-organized/': 'list-checks',
  '/start/signing-in/': 'log-in',

  '/backups/how-backups-work/': 'database-backup',
  '/backups/schedule-and-scope/': 'calendar',
  '/backups/running-a-backup/': 'play',
  '/backups/reading-a-run/': 'history',
  '/backups/retention-and-cleanup/': 'clock',

  '/restore/restoring-a-base/': 'rotate-ccw',
  '/restore/attachments/': 'download',

  '/connections/sources/': 'plug',
  '/connections/destinations/': 'globe',
  '/connections/reconnecting/': 'lock',

  '/schema/': 'table-2',
  '/data/records/': 'search',
  '/data/attachments/': 'download',
  '/data/presets-and-export/': 'square-arrow-out-up-right',

  '/troubleshooting/backup-failed/': 'triangle-alert',
  '/troubleshooting/connection-needs-reconnecting/': 'lock',
  '/troubleshooting/missing-bases/': 'circle-slash',
  '/troubleshooting/what-baseout-cannot-capture/': 'circle-dashed',
};

/**
 * THE PER-PLATFORM HALF OF THE DIRECTORY, resolved against the real content collection.
 *
 * THE LABEL IS THE PAGE'S OWN `title`, read at build time rather than typed here. A link's text is
 * the one thing it cannot afford to be wrong about, and the titles genuinely differ per platform in
 * ways a template would get wrong: Airtable has "field types", Notion has "property types", and
 * ClickUp splits its columns in two and says so in the title.
 *
 * A TOPIC WITH NO PAGE IS SKIPPED rather than rendered dark, so this block cannot mint a dead
 * internal link — which is precisely the defect this app shipped last (`/chat` in the sidebar,
 * 404 on all 41 pages, every other gate green).
 */
const docTitles = new Map<string, string>();
for (const entry of await getCollection('docs')) docTitles.set(entry.id, entry.data.title);

function platformTopics(bandKey: string, id: string) {
  return PLATFORM_TOPICS.filter((t) => t.band === bandKey)
    .map((t) => ({ ...t, title: docTitles.get(`platforms/${id}/${t.slug}`) }))
    .filter((t): t is typeof t & { title: string } => Boolean(t.title));
}

/* THE ROADMAP CARD IS GONE FROM "STILL STUCK?" (Oleh, 2026-08-19), in all three variants, because
   all three carry the roadmap strip: the board is already opened twice above, by three real request
   rows and by the link beside their heading. A third route to the same page, dressed as one of the
   things to do when the documentation failed you, is the page arguing with itself about what the
   reader should do next. */
const EXITS = WAYS_OUT.filter((w) => w.href !== '/roadmap/');

/* AIRTABLE IS ABSENT FROM THIS LIST ON PURPOSE. It is the platform the screenshots ARE, so its
   frame is the screenshot and it needs no substitute. The other two get their own vocabulary in the
   same frame, because there is no ClickUp or Notion product screen and there will not be one: the
   features do not exist yet, and a fabricated screenshot of a feature that does not exist is the
   one mistake a support portal cannot recover from. */
const GLOSSARY_PLATFORMS = PLATFORMS.filter((p) => p.id !== 'airtable');


/* NO COUNT IN THE SUBHEAD. It said "22 pages", which is the number of links this block draws and
   not the number of pages the documentation has (38, and the sidebar shows every one of them). A
   reader who opens the sidebar catches the discrepancy in a second, and a support portal that is
   caught being loose with a number it did not have to print has spent something it needs. */
---

{/* `not-content` is load-bearing, not tidiness. This renders inside `.sl-markdown-content`, whose
    prose rhythm is a GENERAL-SIBLING rule: it puts `margin-top` on every second-and-later child of
    every nested flex or grid box in here. Measured before it was added: steps 02 and 03 carried
    `margin-top: 4px` and step 01 did not, so the row of three sat 4px out of true and the cause was
    nowhere in this file. It is the same rule that made the roadmap board look crooked in August. */}
{/* THE PRE-PAINT STAMP, and it is `is:inline` for the one reason that justifies an unchecked
    script: a module script is deferred, so it runs AFTER this markup has painted, and both the
    treatment switch and the platform re-labelling would be visible flipping. Stamped here, before
    the strip below is even parsed, the browser has the answer at first paint and the stylesheet
    does the rest. `lib/shot-switch.ts` and `lib/landing-strip.ts` are the authority a moment later
    and will correct anything this cheap read got wrong; the two storage keys are repeated verbatim
    because an inline script cannot import them.

    Two platforms selected leaves the stamp OFF, which is the neutral copy: "which Bases or Spaces
    to back up" is not a sentence, and a reader with two platforms on has not told us whose nouns
    are theirs. */}
<script is:inline>
  (function () {
    var r = document.documentElement;
    try {
      var q = new URLSearchParams(window.location.search);
      var s = q.get('shots');
      if (s !== 'filter' && s !== 'reactive') s = window.localStorage.getItem('bo-landing-shots');
      r.dataset.shots = s === 'filter' || s === 'reactive' ? s : 'reactive';

      var p = q.get('platform');
      if (!p) {
        var raw = window.localStorage.getItem('bo-platforms');
        var list = raw ? JSON.parse(raw) : null;
        p = Array.isArray(list) && list.length === 1 ? list[0] : null;
      }
      if (p === 'airtable' || p === 'clickup' || p === 'notion') r.dataset.shotPlatform = p;
    } catch (e) {
      r.dataset.shots = 'reactive';
    }
  })();
</script>

<div class="vb ja not-content">
  <!-- 1 · WHO ARE YOU -->

  <PlatformStart />

  <!-- 2 · THE PATH -->

  <section class="vb-sec vb-sec-lead" aria-labelledby="vb-path-h">
    <div class="vb-head">
      <h2 class="vb-h" id="vb-path-h">Set up a backup</h2>
      <p class="vb-sub">Three steps, once per Space. Each one opens the page that covers it.</p>
    </div>

    <ol class="vb-steps">
      {
        STEPS.map((s) => {
          const parts = bodyParts(s);
          return (
            <li class="vb-step">
              <span class="vb-step-n" aria-hidden="true">{String(s.n).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
              <h3 class="vb-step-title">
                <a class="vb-step-link" href={s.href} data-step-link={String(s.n)}>
                  {s.title}
                  <span class="vb-arrow"><Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} /></span>
                </a>
              </h3>
              <p class="vb-step-body">{parts.before}{nounReadings(s).map((r) => (
                <span class="vb-noun" data-for={r.key}>{r.text}</span>
              ))}{parts.after}</p>
              <span class="vb-shot">
                <img
                  class="vb-shot-light"
                  src={`/screens/${s.shot}-light.png`}
                  alt={s.alt}
                  width="1040"
                  height="650"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />
                <img
                  class="vb-shot-dark"
                  src={`/screens/${s.shot}-dark.png`}
                  alt={s.alt}
                  width="1040"
                  height="650"
                  loading="lazy"
                  decoding="async"
                />
                {GLOSSARY_PLATFORMS.map((p) => (
                  <span class="vb-glo" data-plat={p.id}>
                    <span class="vb-glo-head">
                      <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={16} />
                      <span class="vb-glo-name">{p.name}</span>
                    </span>
                    <span class="vb-glo-rows">
                      {GLOSSARY_ROWS.map((row) => (
                        <span
                          class="vb-glo-row"
                          data-focus={s.glossaryFocus.includes(row.from) ? '' : undefined}
                        >
                          <span class="vb-glo-k">{row.label}</span>
                          <span class="vb-glo-v">{p.vocab[row.from]}</span>
                        </span>
                      ))}
                    </span>
                  </span>
                ))}
              </span>
            </li>
          );
        })
      }
    </ol>
  </section>

  <!-- 3 · THE DIRECTORY -->

  <section class="vb-sec" aria-labelledby="vb-dir-h">
    <div class="vb-head">
      <h2 class="vb-h" id="vb-dir-h">The documentation</h2>
      <p class="vb-sub">Grouped by what you are doing at the time.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="ja-dir">
      {
        BANDS.map((band) => (
          <section class="ja-band">
            <div class="ja-head">
              <h3 class="ja-verb">
                <Icon name={band.icon} size={14} />
                {band.verb}
              </h3>
              <p class="ja-line">{band.line}</p>
            </div>

            <ul class="ja-grid">
              {band.items.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <a class="ja-card" href={item.href}>
                    <span class="ja-ico">
                      <Icon name={CARD_ICON[item.href] ?? band.icon} size={18} />
                    </span>
                    <span class="ja-body">
                      <span class="ja-label">
                        {item.label}
                        {SHOW_DRAFT_FLAGS && !item.written && (
                          <span class="ja-flag">Not written yet</span>
                        )}
                      </span>
                      <span class="ja-blurb">{item.blurb}</span>
                    </span>
                  </a>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>

            {PLATFORMS.map((p) => {
              const topics = platformTopics(band.key, p.id);
              return topics.length ? (
                <div class="ja-plat" data-plat={p.id}>
                  <h4 class="ja-plat-h">
                    <PlatformMark id={p.id} size={14} />
                    Specific to {p.name}
                  </h4>
                  <ul class="ja-grid">
                    {topics.map((t) => (
                      <li>
                        <a class="ja-card" href={`/platforms/${p.id}/${t.slug}/`}>
                          <span class="ja-ico">
                            <Icon name={t.icon} size={18} />
                          </span>
                          <span class="ja-body">
                            <span class="ja-label">{t.title}</span>
                            <span class="ja-blurb">{t.blurb}</span>
                          </span>
                        </a>
                      </li>
                    ))}
                  </ul>
                </div>
              ) : null;
            })}
          </section>
        ))
      }
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- 4 · THE ROADMAP -->

  <section class="vb-sec" aria-labelledby="vb-road-h">
    <div class="vb-head vb-head-row">
      <div>
        <h2 class="vb-h" id="vb-road-h">From the roadmap</h2>
        <p class="vb-sub">One in progress, one shipped, one planned.</p>
      </div>
      <a class="vb-more" href="/roadmap/">
        See the whole roadmap
        <span class="vb-arrow"><Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} /></span>
      </a>
    </div>

    {/* THE BOARD'S OWN CARD, IMPORTED RATHER THAN IMITATED. This strip used to draw its own tile —
        a status badge on top, a title, a summary and a dated line with a calendar glyph — which was
        the fifth copy of a card that had already been written four times and drifted, and the
        extraction of `RequestCard.astro` exists to end exactly that. A landing that shows the
        roadmap in a different shape from the roadmap teaches the reader two cards for one thing.

        AND THE VOTE BUTTON IS WIRED, which is the one real objection to reusing it. `lib/board.ts`
        runs on `/roadmap/` and on every request detail page, and its voting half is deliberately
        UNCONDITIONAL — a bail-out on the missing board once took the vote down on nine detail pages
        and looked wired the whole time, which is why it is written that way. Calling `wireBoard()`
        from this page's script therefore costs nothing but the vote, and a dead control on a
        landing page would have been worse than no control at all. */}
    <ul class="vb-road">
      {ROADMAP_TILES.map(({ item }) => (
        <li>
          <RequestCard r={item} />
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </section>

  <!-- 5 · THE WAYS OUT -->

  <section class="vb-sec" aria-labelledby="vb-out-h">
    <div class="vb-head">
      <h2 class="vb-h" id="vb-out-h">Still stuck?</h2>
    </div>

    <ul class="vb-ways">
      {
        EXITS.map((w) => (
          <li>
            <a class="vb-way" href={w.href} data-open-chat={w.opensChat ? '' : undefined}>
              <span class="vb-way-i">
                <Icon name={w.icon} size={16} />
              </span>
              <span class="vb-way-t">{w.title}</span>
              <span class="vb-way-b">{w.body}</span>
            </a>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
  </section>
</div>

{/* OUTSIDE `.vb`, and last in the document. Outside because `.vb` caps the measure and pads the
    block, and a fixed control has no business inheriting either. Last so it is last in the tab
    order: a demo toggle must not sit between the reader and the page's real links. */}
<ShotSwitch />

<script>
  import { mountLandingStrip } from '../../lib/landing-strip';
  import { wireBoard } from '../../lib/board';
  import { OPEN_CHAT_EVENT } from '../../lib/rows';

  /* The vote control on the three roadmap cards. `wireBoard` does two independent jobs and only the
     filtering half looks for `[data-board]`; the voting half is unconditional by design, so this is
     the supported way to have a working card outside `/roadmap/`. Without it the card would render
     a button that silently does nothing, which is the one honest argument against reusing it. */
  wireBoard();

  /* The strip's reaction to the platform the reader named. It sets one attribute on `<html>`; the
     stylesheet below does the rest. */
  mountLandingStrip();

  /* Scoped to this body rather than the whole document: the chat card is not the only thing on the
     portal that opens the drawer, and a document-wide selector here would fight the others. The
     `[data-landing-body="a"]` wrapper this used to hang off went with the preview switch. */
  for (const el of document.querySelectorAll('.ja [data-open-chat]')) {
    el.addEventListener('click', (e) => {
      e.preventDefault();
      document.dispatchEvent(new CustomEvent(OPEN_CHAT_EVENT, { detail: {} }));
    });
  }
</script>

<style>
  /* ── The frame ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * No inline padding: the Starlight container already supplies it, and adding a second one is how
   * the reading column ends up narrower here than on every other page. `min-width: 0` appears on
   * every grid child below for the one reason it always does: a grid item's default `auto` minimum
   * is its content, and one unbreakable string then pushes the whole page sideways at 390px.
   */
  /* THE ROOT CARRIES BOTH CLASSES ON PURPOSE. This file was made by transplanting sections out of
     another one, and the transplant renamed the root to `ja` while leaving the frame rule keyed to
     `.vb` — so for several rounds A and B rendered with no width cap and no block padding while C
     had both, and the "one variable at a time" comparison was quietly carrying two. Scoped styles
     make that silent: the selector is valid, it simply matches nothing. */
  .vb {
    max-width: 72rem;
    margin-inline: auto;
    padding-block: 32px 64px;
  }

  .vb-sec + .vb-sec {
    margin-top: 56px;
  }

  /* THE ONE SECTION GAP THE SIBLING RULE ABOVE CANNOT REACH, and the client felt it before anyone
     measured it: "Set up a backup" sticks very hard to the three platform cards above it. The
     reason is structural. `PlatformStart` renders its own `<section class="ps">`, so the first
     `.vb-sec` on this page has no `.vb-sec` before it and `+` never fires — measured at 0px between
     the two section boxes, and 8px from the bottom of the card grid to the heading, against 56px
     everywhere else on the page. Named explicitly rather than reached with `:first-of-type`, which
     would match nothing here: `.ps` is also a `section`, so the path is the SECOND one. */
  .vb-sec-lead {
    margin-top: 56px;
  }

  .vb-head {
    margin-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .vb-head-row {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-end;
    justify-content: space-between;
    gap: 16px;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
  }

  .vb-h {
    margin: 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-20);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.3;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .vb-sub {
    margin: 4px 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    line-height: 1.5;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* An arrow that inherits its label's colour. `line-height: 0` on the wrapper is not cosmetic:
     an inline box carrying an svg otherwise contributes its own line box and inflates the line it
     sits in, which is exactly the bug that shipped on the header once already. */
  .vb-arrow {
    display: inline-block;
    line-height: 0;
    margin-left: 4px;
    vertical-align: -0.15em;
  }

  .vb-more {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-14);
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--bo-primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-4);
    transition: opacity 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .vb-more:hover {
    opacity: 0.8;
  }

  .vb-more:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
  }

  /* ── 1 · The path ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * THE THREE COLUMNS MUST BE THE SAME WIDTH, and the first version was not: asymmetric cell
   * padding (`0 24px` on the ends, `24px` both sides plus a border in the middle) left the middle
   * content box 25px narrower than the first, so the middle SHOT rendered 16px shorter and the row
   * lost the shared baseline it was built for. Measured at 1440: 360 · 334 · 358.
   *
   * So the breathing room is a `column-gap` and the dividers are pseudo-elements sitting in it,
   * which take no width from the track. The unbroken rule across the top was never at risk from a
   * gap: `border-top` is on the LIST, not on the cells, so it is one line whatever the columns do.
   */
  .vb-steps {
    list-style: none;
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: repeat(3, minmax(0, 1fr));
    column-gap: 40px;
    row-gap: 0;
    border-top: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* `position: relative` is here so the title link can stretch over the whole column, screenshot
     included: one link in the accessibility tree, one Tab stop, a target the size of the step. */
  .vb-step {
    position: relative;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    min-width: 0;
    padding: 16px 0 0;
  }

  /* The divider, drawn in the middle of the gap so no track pays for it. It starts below the top
     rule rather than at it, so the two hairlines meet as a T rather than crossing. */
  .vb-step + .vb-step::before {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    top: 16px;
    bottom: 0;
    left: -20px;
    width: 1px;
    background: var(--bo-base-300);
  }

  /* 11px is the floor, and only ever here: an uppercase tracked micro-label, never prose. */
  .vb-step-n {
    display: block;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-11);
    font-weight: 600;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    color: var(--bo-ornament);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }

  .vb-step-title {
    margin: 8px 0 0;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-16);
    font-weight: 600;
    line-height: 1.35;
    color: var(--bo-base-content);
  }

  .vb-step-link {
    color: inherit;
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: color 150ms ease-out;
  }

  .vb-step-link::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
  }

  .vb-step-body {
    margin: 8px 0 20px;
    font-size: var(--bo-t-13);
    line-height: 1.55;
    color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  /* `margin-top: auto` is what puts three shots on one baseline without a fixed body height: the
     column is a flex column, so the last child absorbs whatever the sentence above did not use. */
  .vb-shot {
    display: block;
    position: relative;
    margin-top: auto;
    aspect-ratio: 16 / 10;
    overflow: hidden;
    border: 1px solid var(--bo-base-300);
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
    background: var(--bo-base-200);
    transition: border-color 200ms ease-out;
  }

  .vb-shot img {
    width: 100%;
    height: 100%;
    object-fit: cover;
    object-position: top left;
  }

  .vb-step:hover .vb-shot,
  .vb-step:focus-within .vb-shot {
    border-color: var(--bo-muted);
  }

  .vb-step:hover .vb-step-link,
  .vb-step:focus-within .vb-step-link {
    color: var(--bo-primary);
  }

  .vb-step-link:focus-visible::after {
    outline: 2px solid var(--bo-primary);
    outline-offset: -1px;
    border-radius: var(--bo-r-8);
  }

  /* ── The theme swap ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
   * A raster cannot follow a theme, so both exist and one is hidden. Three states have to be right,
   * not two: Starlight stamps `data-theme="light" | "dark"` on the root when the reader chooses,
   * and stamps NOTHING on the default setting, where only `prefers-color-scheme` separates them.
   * Hence the media query is guarded with `:not([data-theme='light'])` rather than left bare: an OS
   * in dark mode plus an explicit light choice is a real combination, and a bare media query hands
   * that reader the dark screenshot on a white page.
   *
   * `display`, not `content-visibility`: the latter does not remove the box, it defers its
   * rendering, so both shots would still occupy the frame.
